Settle an audio stream the probe never saw at its first packet.

avformat_find_stream_info learns a stream's parameters by decoding its first packets. A stream
that has no packet within the probe, because a transport stream lists a sound that starts later
or starts carrying one later on, as a multicast, a channel joined between programmes or a tuner
recording does, never had that done. For AAC the parser deliberately leaves the rate and the
channels unset, because an ADTS header misstates them for HE-AAC, so such a stream stayed at no
rate and no channels for as long as it played. With no rate, compute_pkt_fields could not give
its packets a duration, and could not date the packets after the first in each PES packet, which
carry no timestamp of their own, so about four packets in five came out undated.

Once avformat_find_stream_info has returned, the first packet of an audio stream with no sample
rate is now decoded with a throwaway decoder, as that function would have done, and the stream's
rate, channel layout, sample format and frame size are taken from the frame before the packet's
fields are computed. The stream then reads as one the probe saw: its parameters are known, every
packet has a duration, and an undated packet is dated from the one before it. A packet that does
not decode leaves the stream as it was, and the next is tried, up to eight. The packet that settles
the stream takes the decoded frame's length when it has none, because its codec can still be the
programme table's guess: a table names MPEG audio MP3, an MP3 packet at a low rate is timed as 576
samples, and an MP2 frame holds 1152; the parser corrects the codec from the next packet on.
Streams the probe saw,
and every stream of a caller that never calls avformat_find_stream_info, are untouched.

Applied by BuildFFmpegTask before configure.

